WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.889 --> 00:00:06.370
Hi everyone, and welcome to Biology
Professor. Today, we're going to be

00:00:06.370 --> 00:00:13.230
talking about the Acid Fast Stain. Now, in
the laboratory, when trying to identify

00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:19.800
different types of bacteria in a given
sample, the most common type of

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:25.269
differential stain used is called the
Gram Stain. It differentiates between

00:00:25.269 --> 00:00:30.160
gram-positive and gram-negative cells
based on their cell wall characteristics.

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:34.840
If you're interested in learning more
about that, you can either see my video

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:39.100
on the gram positive versus
gram-negative cells or you can also see

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:46.300
my video on the Gram Stain procedure. Now interestingly, there are some types of

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:51.160
bacteria that they just can't be
distinguished with the Gram Stain. These

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:56.699
are called gram-indeterminate bacteria.
For example, there are some types of

00:00:56.699 --> 00:01:02.859
bacteria that they have gram positive
cell walls, meaning that they have that

00:01:02.859 --> 00:01:09.280
characteristic thick peptidoglycan layer
covering a plasma membrane and they lack

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:13.030
gram-negative cell characteristics, for
example they don't have the outer

00:01:13.030 --> 00:01:18.460
membrane and they don't have any LPS, but they're gram-indeterminate, they just

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:23.280
don't stain well with the Gram Stain. The
reason for this is that these bacteria

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:28.770
contain something in their cell wall
called mycolic acid

00:01:36.159 --> 00:01:41.619
and bacteria that have mycolic acid in
their cell wall are referred to as acid

00:01:41.619 --> 00:01:46.420
fast bacteria and they can be
distinguished from non acid fast

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:53.909
bacteria by the Acid Fast stain. The
mycolic acid component is this really

00:01:53.909 --> 00:02:00.939
waxy lipid component it is in the cell
wall and it's what prevents the Gram

00:02:00.939 --> 00:02:05.619
stain reagents from accurately staining
these bacteria, however, there is another

00:02:05.619 --> 00:02:20.260
dye that can be used with the Acid
Fast stain called carbolfuchsin.

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:25.269
Carbolfuchsinis a red dye, it's used first and it will actually dye all of the bacteria

00:02:25.269 --> 00:02:52.000
in a sample this red color. The next step
is the use of acid alcohol.

00:02:53.300 --> 00:03:01.600
Acid alcohol is a decolorizer. For non acid fast bacteria, treatment of acid alcohol will

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:06.910
make the red carbolfuchsin dye come out
of the cell wall so that the cell wall

00:03:06.910 --> 00:03:16.060
is colorless, however carbolfuchsin is
much more soluble in mycolic acid than

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:22.239
it is an acid alcohol. This means that
despite the use of acid alcohol, acid

00:03:22.239 --> 00:03:34.569
fast bacteria that have all this mycolic
acid, they stay red. The final step of the

00:03:34.569 --> 00:03:40.599
acid fast stain is to use a counter stain.
This is just some stain that will allow

00:03:40.599 --> 00:03:44.919
the non acid fast bacteria to be
visualized a little better.

00:03:44.919 --> 00:03:49.130
The most common counter stain used with acid fast stains is methylene blue.

00:03:49.130 --> 00:04:10.640
Methylene blue will stain non
acid-fast bacteria blue while the

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:14.900
acid-fast bacteria still have all of
these carbolfuchsin dye,

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:26.890
thus they remain red. So now under a
microscope, acid-fast bacteria can be

00:04:26.890 --> 00:04:32.270
differentiated from non-acid fast
bacteria with this stain.

00:04:32.270 --> 00:04:37.970
A very common pathogen that is
identified by use of the acid fast stain

00:04:37.970 --> 00:04:44.360
are species of Mycobacterium. These
include Mycobacterium tuberculosis which

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:48.080
causes tuberculosis, also Mycobacterium
leprae

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:54.530
which causes leprosy, so this is the main
stain for identifying those pathogens

00:04:54.530 --> 00:04:57.820
in human samples.

